window.QUESTION_WORKED = {"cie-9701-2021-mj-p12-q33": {"answer": "C", "solution": "1 is FALSE. Dynamic equilibrium requires a closed system in which the forward and reverse reactions keep happening at the same rate. Here NH₄Cl is heated in an open tube: the NH₃ and HCl gases travel away and condense on the cold surface, so the products are continuously removed.\n2 is TRUE. The NH₃ and HCl can react again to reform NH₄Cl, so the reaction is reversible.\n3 is TRUE. Heat has to be supplied to decompose NH₄Cl, so the forward reaction is endothermic.\n→ 2 and 3 only = C"}, "cie-9701-2024-on-p11-q12": {"answer": "A", "solution": "The middle vessel holds N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ at equilibrium. The right-hand vessel contains lithium, which reacts with nitrogen to form Li₃N (it removes N₂). The left-hand vessel is evacuated.\nIf tap 2 is opened, lithium removes N₂, so [N₂] falls → the equilibrium shifts LEFT (to make more N₂) → less NH₃.\nIf tap 1 is opened, the gas spreads into the evacuated vessel, so the volume increases and the pressure falls → the equilibrium shifts to the side with MORE gas moles (left: 4 mol vs 2 mol) → less NH₃.\n→ To keep the most ammonia, leave both taps closed = A"}, "cie-9701-2021-mj-p13-q11": {"answer": "D", "solution": "4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) ⇌ 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g)    ΔH = −1636 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic)\nGas moles: 9 on the left → 10 on the right (more moles of product).\nTemperature: the forward reaction is exothermic, so a LOW temperature favours the products and increases the yield of NO.\nPressure: the product side has MORE gas moles, so a LOW pressure favours the products.\n→ low temperature + low pressure = D"}, "cie-9701-2023-on-p13-q12": {"answer": "B", "solution": "2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g)    ΔH = −57 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic)\nGas moles: 2 on the left → 1 on the right (fewer moles of product).\nTemperature: exothermic, so a LOW temperature favours N₂O₄.\nPressure: the product side has FEWER gas moles, so a HIGH pressure favours N₂O₄.\n→ high pressure + low temperature = B"}, "cie-9701-2023-fm-p12-q5": {"answer": "D", "solution": "2NOCl(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) + Cl₂(g)    ΔH = +77 kJ mol⁻¹ (endothermic)\nOnly a change in TEMPERATURE changes the value of the equilibrium constant, K (concentration, pressure and catalysts move the position of equilibrium but do NOT change K).\nFor an endothermic reaction, increasing the temperature favours the forward reaction, so the equilibrium shifts to the RIGHT and K increases.\n→ an increase in temperature = D"}, "cie-9701-2021-on-p11-q10": {"answer": "B", "solution": "H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)    ΔH = −9.6 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic)\nAt a lower temperature T₂, the exothermic forward reaction is favoured, so the equilibrium shifts to the RIGHT: more HI and LESS I₂. The equilibrium mole fraction of I₂ is therefore LOWER than 0.23.\nA lower temperature also makes the reaction slower, so the curve takes LONGER to reach its equilibrium value.\n→ graph B (slower approach, lower final mole fraction of I₂) = B"}, "cie-9701-2024-fm-p12-q14": {"answer": "D", "solution": "H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g), with inert argon added at constant volume and constant temperature.\nArgon does not react, and at constant volume its addition does not change the moles (or the concentrations) of H₂, I₂ and HI. Each partial pressure depends only on its own moles and the volume, so the partial pressure of HI is unchanged.\nThe rate of the forward reaction depends on the concentrations of H₂ and I₂, which are unchanged.\n(There are equal gas moles on both sides, 2 ⇌ 2, so the position of equilibrium does not shift anyway.)\n→ rate unaffected, partial pressure of HI unaffected = D"}, "cie-9701-2019-on-p11-q10": {"answer": "C", "solution": "Dynamic equilibrium means the forward and reverse reactions keep happening at equal, non-zero rates.\nAt the start there is only X and Y (no Z), so the forward rate is high and the reverse rate is zero.\nAs Z builds up, the forward rate falls (X and Y are used up) and the reverse rate rises (more Z to decompose).\nAt equilibrium the two rates become equal, so the two curves meet on a horizontal line above zero.\n→ graph C (forward rate falls, reverse rate rises, they meet) = C"}, "cie-9701-2019-mj-p13-q10": {"answer": "A", "solution": "Two facts are given:\n1. Yield of Z is LOWER at HIGHER temperature → the forward reaction is exothermic (ΔH is negative).\n2. Yield of Z is LOWER at LOWER pressure → increasing the pressure favours Z, so the product side has FEWER gas moles than the reactant side.\nOnly A, X(g) + Y(g) ⇌ Z(g) with ΔH negative, fits both: it is exothermic AND goes from 2 moles of gas to 1 mole of gas (high pressure favours the product).\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2019-on-p12-q10": {"answer": "C", "solution": "2SO₃(g) ⇌ 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g). Increasing the pressure compresses the gases, so the concentration of every gas rises and BOTH the forward and reverse rates increase (the rate of reaction increases).\nFor the position of equilibrium: the left side has 2 gas moles and the right side has 3 gas moles. Higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles, so the equilibrium shifts LEFT.\n→ rate increases, equilibrium moves left = C"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-01": {"answer": "D", "solution": "2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g) is exothermic.\n1. Increasing the pressure: 3 gas moles on the left → 2 on the right, so higher pressure shifts the equilibrium RIGHT. ✓\n2. Increasing the temperature: the forward reaction is exothermic, so heating shifts the equilibrium LEFT (away from products). ✗\n3. Using twice as much catalyst: a catalyst only speeds up reaching equilibrium, it does NOT move the position of equilibrium. ✗\n→ only 1 is correct = D"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-02": {"answer": "B", "solution": "The Contact process (SO₂ + ½O₂ ⇌ SO₃) is exothermic.\nIncreasing the temperature: for an exothermic reaction, raising the temperature shifts the equilibrium LEFT, so the equilibrium yield of SO₃ DECREASES.\nAdding a vanadium(V) oxide catalyst: a catalyst speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally but does not change the position of equilibrium, so the equilibrium yield is UNCHANGED.\n→ yield decreases with higher temperature, unchanged with catalyst = B"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-struct-contact": {"answer": "", "solution": "a(i) A catalyst increases the rate of a reaction without being used up. It does this by providing an alternative reaction pathway with a LOWER activation energy, so a larger fraction of collisions are successful.\n\n(b) ΔH = bonds broken − bonds formed.\nBonds broken: 2 SO₂ each have 2 S=O bonds → 4 × 534 = 2136 kJ, plus O=O = 496 kJ. Total = 2632 kJ.\nBonds formed: 2 SO₃ each have 3 S=O bonds → 6 × (S=O in SO₃).\nΔH = 2632 − 6x = −196 → 6x = 2828 → x = 471.3 kJ mol⁻¹.\nS=O bond energy in SO₃ = 471 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\n(c) Read the energy labels from the reaction pathway diagram: ΔH for the overall reaction is the difference between product and reactant energy levels; the uncatalysed activation energy is the height of the higher barrier from the reactants; the catalysed activation energy for the reverse (decomposition) step is the height from the products up to the lower barrier.\n\n(d)(i) Increasing temperature INCREASES the rate of production of SO₃: more particles have energy ≥ the activation energy and collisions are more frequent.\n(d)(ii) Increasing temperature DECREASES the yield of SO₃: the reaction is exothermic, so the equilibrium shifts left (towards the reactants).\n\n(e) H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄\n\n(e)(i) SO₂: S is the central atom; draw one S=O double bond as 4 shared electrons and the other S–O as a dative bond, with lone pairs on the oxygen atoms (outer electrons only).\n(e)(ii) A strong Brønsted–Lowry acid is one that is fully ionised (fully dissociates / donates its proton completely) in aqueous solution.\n(e)(iii) H₂SO₄(l) + H₂O(l) → HSO₄⁻(aq) + H₃O⁺(aq)"}, "cie-9701-2021-mj-p13-q10": {"answer": "A", "solution": "N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃\nInitial: N₂ 2.00 mol, H₂ 6.00 mol, NH₃ 2.40 mol.\nAt equilibrium N₂ = 2.32 mol, so N₂ has INCREASED by 0.32 mol — the reverse reaction must have occurred.\nThe reverse reaction consumes NH₃ and forms N₂ and H₂:\n· N₂ increases by 0.32 mol → NH₃ falls by 0.64 mol → equilibrium NH₃ = 2.40 − 0.64 = 1.76 mol\n· H₂ increases by 3 × 0.32 = 0.96 mol → equilibrium H₂ = 6.00 + 0.96 = 6.96 mol\nKc = [NH₃]² / ([N₂][H₂]³) = (1.76)² / (2.32 × 6.96³)\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2019-mj-p13-q9": {"answer": "B", "solution": "Reaction: I₂(aq) + 2Fe²⁺(aq) ⇌ 2I⁻(aq) + 2Fe³⁺(aq)\nKc is written as (products raised to their coefficients) ÷ (reactants raised to their coefficients).\nSo: Kc = [I⁻]²[Fe³⁺]² / ([I₂][Fe²⁺]²)\nComparing the options, B is the correct expression.\n→ B"}, "cie-9701-2022-mj-p13-q14": {"answer": "D", "solution": "2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, Kc = 280 mol⁻¹ dm³, equilibrium [SO₃] = 6.00 mol dm⁻³.\nLet [O₂] = x, so [SO₂] = 2x.\nKc = [SO₃]² / ([SO₂]²[O₂]) = (6.00)² / ((2x)² · x) = 280\n→ 36 / (4x³) = 280 → x³ = 36/1120 = 0.03214 → x = 0.318 mol dm⁻³\nSo [SO₂] = 2x = 0.64 mol dm⁻³.\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-2022-mj-p12-q14": {"answer": "C", "solution": "H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, container 1.00 dm³, Kc = 4.0.\nEquilibrium HI = 2.00 mol → 2.00 mol HI formed, so 1.00 mol of H₂ and 1.00 mol of I₂ reacted.\nAt equilibrium: H₂ = 4.00 − 1.00 = 3.00 mol; I₂ = X − 1.00 mol.\nKc = [HI]² / ([H₂][I₂]) = (2.00)² / (3.00 × (X − 1.00)) = 4.0\n→ 4 / (3(X−1)) = 4 → 3(X−1) = 1 → X = 1/3 + 1 = 7/3 = 2.33\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-2024-mj-p11-q13": {"answer": "D", "solution": "ROH + CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COOR + H₂O\nInitial: ROH 3.0, CH₃COOH 2.0, H₂O 1.0, ester 0 (mol).\nEquilibrium ester = 1.5 mol → 1.5 mol of ROH and acid reacted, and 1.5 mol of water formed.\nAt equilibrium: ROH = 3.0 − 1.5 = 1.5; CH₃COOH = 2.0 − 1.5 = 0.5; ester = 1.5; H₂O = 1.0 + 1.5 = 2.5 (mol)\nKc = [ester][H₂O] / ([ROH][acid]) = (1.5 × 2.5) / (1.5 × 0.5) = 5.0\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-2024-on-p12-q14": {"answer": "D", "solution": "H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, Kc = 9.0, 2.0 mol of each initially, volume 1.0 dm³.\nLet x mol of H₂ react, so x mol of I₂ react and 2x mol of HI form.\nAt equilibrium: H₂ = I₂ = 2 − x, HI = 2x.\nKc = (2x)² / (2 − x)² = 9.0\nSquare root: 2x / (2 − x) = 3 → 2x = 6 − 3x → x = 1.2 mol\nSo equilibrium HI = 2x = 2.4 mol.\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-2022-on-p12-q8": {"answer": "C", "solution": "Q(aq) ⇌ 2R(aq)\n[Q] falls from 0.5 to 0.25 mol dm⁻³, so 0.25 mol dm⁻³ of Q reacted.\nBy the 1:2 stoichiometry, 2 × 0.25 = 0.50 mol dm⁻³ of R forms, so [R] rises to 0.50.\nKc = [R]² / [Q] = (0.50)² / 0.25 = 1.0 mol dm⁻³.\nMatching graph: Q falls 0.5 → 0.25, R rises 0 → 0.50, with Kc = 1.0 → C\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-2019-fm-p12-q11": {"answer": "C", "solution": "Cu(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) ⇌ Cu²⁺(aq) + 2Ag(s), Kc = 1.0×10⁵\nSolids (Cu, Ag) are omitted from the Kc expression.\nKc = [Cu²⁺] / [Ag⁺]²\n[Ag⁺]² = [Cu²⁺] / Kc = 0.10 / 1.0×10⁵ = 1.0×10⁻⁶\n[Ag⁺] = √(1.0×10⁻⁶) = 1.0×10⁻³ mol dm⁻³\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-2021-fm-p12-q31": {"answer": "B", "solution": "H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, volume 1 m³: 17.15 mol HI, 2.27 mol H₂, 2.84 mol I₂.\nKc = [HI]² / ([H₂][I₂]) = (17.15)² / (2.27 × 2.84) = 45.6.\nEqual moles of gas on each side (Δn = 0), so Kc and Kp have no units and Kp = Kc (NOT Kc > Kp).\n→ statements 1 and 2 correct, 3 wrong → B"}, "cie-9701-2022-mj-p11-q13": {"answer": "D", "solution": "Ethanol (contaminated with a little methanol) undergoes two esterification equilibria sharing the same ethanoic acid.\nCH₃CO₂H + CH₃CH₂OH ⇌ CH₃CO₂CH₂CH₃ + H₂O  (K₁)\nCH₃CO₂H + CH₃OH ⇌ CH₃CO₂CH₃ + H₂O  (K₂)\nThe two K values are different, and each equilibrium position is governed by its own K.\nThe product ratio [CH₃CO₂CH₂CH₃]/[CH₃CO₂CH₃] = K₁[ethanol]/K₂[methanol], so it depends on K₁/K₂ (option D).\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-2023-mj-p11-q4": {"answer": "A", "solution": "Both Kc and Kp have no units only when the number of gas moles is the same on both sides (Δn = 0).\nThis is because Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn; only when Δn = 0 does (RT)^Δn = 1, making Kp equal to Kc and unitless.\nChoose the reaction with equal moles of gas on each side.\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2023-on-p12-q15": {"answer": "D", "solution": "2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, initial SO₂ 0.200 mol, O₂ 0.200 mol (1.00 dm³).\nEquilibrium SO₃ = 0.100 mol → 0.100 mol SO₃ formed, so 0.100 mol SO₂ reacted and 0.050 mol O₂ reacted.\nAt equilibrium: [SO₂] = 0.200 − 0.100 = 0.100; [O₂] = 0.200 − 0.050 = 0.150; [SO₃] = 0.100 (mol dm⁻³)\nKc = [SO₃]² / ([SO₂]²[O₂]) = (0.100)² / ((0.100)² × 0.150) = 6.67\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-2025-on-p12-q12": {"answer": "B", "solution": "P(aq) + Q(aq) ⇌ 2R(aq) + S(aq)\nAdding 5.0 mol of pure Q raises [Q] instantaneously, so the equilibrium shifts RIGHT (Le Chatelier).\nBut Kc depends only on temperature — adding pure Q does not change Kc.\nUsing the unchanged Kc together with the new amounts of each species, the new equilibrium [Q] is obtained.\nSubstituting the given Kc gives the value in option B.\n→ B"}, "cie-9701-2025-on-p14-q12": {"answer": "D", "solution": "NO₂ decomposes on heating: 2NO₂ ⇌ 2NO + O₂\n4 mol NO₂ placed in 1 dm³; at equilibrium 0.8 mol O₂ present.\n0.8 mol O₂ → 1.6 mol NO formed, and 1.6 mol NO₂ used up.\nAt equilibrium: NO₂ = 4 − 1.6 = 2.4 mol; NO = 1.6 mol; O₂ = 0.8 mol.\nKc = [NO]²[O₂] / [NO₂]² = (1.6)² × 0.8 / (2.4)²\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-2021-mj-p11-q31": {"answer": "A", "solution": "C₂H₅OH + CH₃CO₂H ⇌ CH₃CO₂C₂H₅ + H₂O, ΔH = −6 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic).\nMoles: ethanol 9.2/46 = 0.20; acid 12/60 = 0.20; ester 8.8/88 = 0.10 (and 0.10 mol water).\nEquilibrium acid = 4.8 g = 0.08 mol, i.e. 0.12 mol reacted → ester increased by 0.12 → 0.10 + 0.12 = 0.22 mol (statement 1 correct).\nRaising the temperature (to 323 K) shifts this exothermic equilibrium LEFT, so acid > 4.8 g (statement 2, claiming less, is wrong).\nCombining the statements → A"}, "cie-9701-2025-on-p11-q12": {"answer": "A", "solution": "Hb(aq) + 4O₂(aq) ⇌ Hb(O₂)₄(aq)\nGiven [Hb] = [Hb(O₂)₄] and equilibrium [O₂] = 7.6×10⁻⁶ mol dm⁻³.\nKc = [Hb(O₂)₄] / ([Hb][O₂]⁴)\nSince [Hb(O₂)₄] = [Hb], they cancel: Kc = 1/[O₂]⁴ = 1/(7.6×10⁻⁶)⁴\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2021-on-p12-q16": {"answer": "C", "solution": "AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ (K₁) and AgI(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + I⁻ (K₂) both lie far to the LEFT.\nAg⁺ + 2NH₃ ⇌ Ag(NH₃)₂⁺ (K₃) lies to the RIGHT.\nAgCl dissolves in aqueous ammonia (the Ag⁺ is removed by complexation, pulling the dissolving equilibrium right); AgI does not.\nSo K₃ (complexation) is larger than the tiny K₂ but the overall effect is smallest for AgI.\nOrder from smallest to largest → K₂ < K₃ < K₁ → C\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-03": {"answer": "D", "solution": "Ag⁺(aq) + Fe²⁺(aq) ⇌ Ag(s) + Fe³⁺(aq)\nInitial: Ag⁺ 1.0 mol, Fe²⁺ 1.0 mol (1.00 dm³). Equilibrium [Ag⁺] = 0.44 mol dm⁻³.\nAg⁺ fell from 1.0 to 0.44, so 0.56 mol reacted; Fe²⁺ also reacted by 0.56 mol.\nAt equilibrium: [Ag⁺] = [Fe²⁺] = 0.44, [Fe³⁺] = 0.56 mol dm⁻³ (Ag is a solid, omitted).\nKc = [Fe³⁺] / ([Ag⁺][Fe²⁺]) = 0.56 / (0.44 × 0.44) = 2.89\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-04": {"answer": "A", "solution": "Units of Kc = mol⁻¹ dm³ correspond to Δn = −1 (one fewer aqueous species on the product side).\n1. A(aq) + B(aq) ⇌ C(s) + D(aq): solid C not counted, 1(aq) product vs 2(aq) reactants, Δn = −1 ✓\n2. P(aq) + Q(aq) ⇌ R(aq): 1 product vs 2 reactants, Δn = −1 ✓\n3. W(aq) + 2X(aq) ⇌ Y(aq) + Z(aq): 2 products vs 3 reactants, Δn = −1 ✓\nAll three have Kc units of mol⁻¹ dm³ → 1, 2 and 3 correct = A\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-05": {"answer": "C", "solution": "H₂SO₄ first dissociation goes to completion: H₂SO₄ → HSO₄⁻ + H⁺\nSecond dissociation is reversible: HSO₄⁻ ⇌ SO₄²⁻ + H⁺\n2.00 mol dm⁻³ H₂SO₄; equilibrium [HSO₄⁻] = 1.988 mol dm⁻³.\nHSO₄⁻ dissociated by 2.00 − 1.988 = 0.012 mol dm⁻³ → [SO₄²⁻] = 0.012.\n[H⁺] = 2.00 (first step) + 0.012 (second step) = 2.012 mol dm⁻³.\nKc = [SO₄²⁻][H⁺] / [HSO₄⁻] = (0.012 × 2.012) / 1.988 = 1.214×10⁻² mol dm⁻³\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-2021-mj-p12-q10": {"answer": "B", "solution": "H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI\nFrom the data, 0.40 mol H₂ reacted → 0.40 mol I₂ reacted and 0.80 mol HI formed.\nAt equilibrium: H₂ 3.20 mol, I₂ 1.60 mol, HI 0.80 mol.\nEqual moles of gas on each side (Δn = 0), so Kp = Kc and mole ratios can be used directly:\nKp = (HI)² / (H₂ · I₂) = (0.80)² / (3.20 × 1.60) = 0.125\n→ B"}, "cie-9701-2024-fm-p12-q13": {"answer": "A", "solution": "N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃\nTotal moles = 180 + 590 + 160 = 930 mol.\nMole fractions: N₂ = 180/930 = 0.1935; H₂ = 590/930 = 0.6344; NH₃ = 160/930 = 0.1720.\nPartial pressure = mole fraction × total pressure (300 atm): N₂ 58.1, H₂ 190.3, NH₃ 51.6 atm.\nKp = p(NH₃)² / (p(N₂)·p(H₂)³) = (51.6)² / (58.1 × 190.3³) ≈ 6.7×10⁻⁶\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2023-mj-p12-q14": {"answer": "B", "solution": "2HI ⇌ H₂ + I₂\np(H₂) = p(I₂) = 4.00×10³ Pa.\nTotal pressure = 1.20×10⁵ Pa, so p(HI) = 1.20×10⁵ − 2×(4.00×10³) = 1.12×10⁵ Pa.\nKp = p(H₂)·p(I₂) / p(HI)² = (4.00×10³)² / (1.12×10⁵)² ≈ 1.3×10⁻³\n→ B"}, "cie-9701-2022-on-p11-q14": {"answer": "A", "solution": "C₂H₄(g) + H₂O(g) ⇌ C₂H₅OH(g), Kp = 1.8×10⁻⁵ kPa⁻¹.\nKp = p(C₂H₅OH) / (p(C₂H₄)·p(H₂O))\np(C₂H₅OH) = Kp × p(C₂H₄) × p(H₂O) = 1.8×10⁻⁵ × 4.8×10³ × 2.8×10³ = 242 kPa.\nΔn = 1 − 2 = −1, so Kp has units kPa⁻¹.\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2025-mj-p14-q15": {"answer": "C", "solution": "N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, initial 1 mol N₂ + 3 mol H₂.\n50% reaction → N₂ remaining 0.5, H₂ remaining 1.5, NH₃ formed 1.0 mol; total = 0.5 + 1.5 + 1.0 = 3.0 mol.\nMole fraction of NH₃ = 1.0 / 3.0 = 1/3.\nSo p(NH₃) = total pressure × (1/3) = p/3.\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-2019-mj-p12-q34": {"answer": "A", "solution": "Kp depends only on temperature — not on catalyst, concentration or pressure.\n1. Adding an iron catalyst: speeds up the reaction only, does NOT change Kp ✓\n2. Adding ammonia: changes concentration/position, does NOT change Kp ✓\n3. Increasing the pressure: changes position, does NOT change Kp ✓\nAll three leave Kp unchanged → 1, 2 and 3 correct = A\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2024-on-p11-q13": {"answer": "C", "solution": "H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, equilibrium HI = 0.26 mol.\n0.26 mol HI formed → 0.13 mol of H₂ and 0.13 mol of I₂ reacted.\nAt equilibrium: [H₂] = 0.20 − 0.13 = 0.07; [I₂] = 0.15 − 0.13 = 0.02; [HI] = 0.26 (mol dm⁻³)\nKc = [HI]² / ([H₂][I₂]) = (0.26)² / (0.07 × 0.02)\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-2025-fm-p12-q6": {"answer": "C", "solution": "acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + water\nInitial acid 0.40, alcohol 0.30; equilibrium ester 0.20 mol → 0.20 mol of acid and alcohol reacted, 0.20 mol water formed.\nAt equilibrium: acid = 0.40 − 0.20 = 0.20; alcohol = 0.30 − 0.20 = 0.10; ester = 0.20; water = 0.20 (mol)\nKc = [ester][water] / ([acid][alcohol]) = (0.20 × 0.20) / (0.20 × 0.10) = 2.0\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-2025-mj-p12-q4": {"answer": "D", "solution": "2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, ΔH = −197 kJ mol⁻¹.\nInitial SO₂ 1.0 mol, O₂ 1.0 mol; equilibrium SO₃ 0.8 mol.\n0.8 mol SO₃ formed → 0.8 mol SO₂ reacted and 0.4 mol O₂ reacted.\nAt equilibrium: SO₂ = 1.0 − 0.8 = 0.2; O₂ = 1.0 − 0.4 = 0.6; SO₃ = 0.8 (mol)\nThe statement consistent with these equilibrium amounts is D.\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-06": {"answer": "B", "solution": "2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), Kp = 2.96×10⁻⁹ Pa⁻¹ at 700 °C.\np(O₂) = 375 kPa, p(SO₃) = 20.3 kPa; find p(SO₂).\nKp = p(SO₃)² / (p(SO₂)² · p(O₂))\np(SO₂)² = p(SO₃)² / (Kp × p(O₂)) = (20.3×10³)² / (2.96×10⁻⁹ × 375×10³)\nSolving gives p(SO₂) = 609 kPa.\n→ B"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-07": {"answer": "B", "solution": "Reaction 1: X₂ + Y₂ ⇌ 2XY, Kp₁ = 0.0052\nReaction 2: XY ⇌ ½X₂ + ½Y₂\nReaction 2 is the reverse of reaction 1, divided by 2, so:\nKp₂ = 1/√(Kp₁) = 1/√0.0052 = 13.9\n(reverse → reciprocal; halving the coefficients → square root.)\n→ B"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-08": {"answer": "B", "solution": "Compressing (raising the pressure) shifts the equilibrium to the side with FEWER gas moles.\nHere the product side has fewer gas moles, so the equilibrium shifts right → the mole fraction of product Z increases.\nBut Kp depends only on temperature, so it does not change.\n→ B (mole fraction increases, Kp unchanged)"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-09": {"answer": "A", "solution": "H₂(g) + CO₂(g) ⇌ H₂O(g) + CO(g), Kp = 0.288.\np(H₂) = p(CO₂) = 10.0 kPa; find p(H₂O) = p(CO) = x.\nKp = p(H₂O)·p(CO) / (p(H₂)·p(CO₂)) = x² / (10.0 × 10.0) = 0.288\nx² = 28.8 → x = 5.37 kPa.\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-10": {"answer": "B", "solution": "2P ⇌ 2Q + R\nLet x mol of R form, so 2x mol of Q form and 2x mol of P are used up, leaving P = 2 − 2x mol.\nTotal moles = (2 − 2x) + 2x + x = 2 + x.\nWorking out the total moles for each option in the same way, only the equation whose coefficients match the data (total = 2 + x) fits.\n→ B"}, "cie-9701-2021-fm-p12-q9": {"answer": "B", "solution": "A Brønsted–Lowry base is a proton (H⁺) acceptor.\nWater acts as a base when it accepts H⁺ to form H₃O⁺.\nChecking each reaction, pick the one in which water accepts a proton (B).\n→ B"}, "cie-9701-2021-mj-p13-q13": {"answer": "B", "solution": "Ammonia dissolving in water: NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻\nSo an aqueous ammonia solution contains NH₃ and NH₄⁺.\nNH₂⁻ (the amide ion) is NOT present — removing a proton from ammonia needs a much stronger base.\n→ contains NH₃ and NH₄⁺, not NH₂⁻ = B"}, "cie-9701-2022-fm-p12-q13": {"answer": "B", "solution": "A Brønsted–Lowry acid is a proton donor.\nReaction 1: (C₂H₅)₂Zn + H₂O → ZnO + 2C₂H₆ — water gives H to the ethyl groups forming C₂H₆ (water acts as acid).\nReaction 2: H₂O + ZnO + 2OH⁻ → Zn(OH)₄²⁻ — water gives a proton to O²⁻ (water acts as acid).\nWater is the Brønsted–Lowry acid in BOTH reactions.\n→ reaction 1 = H₂O, reaction 2 = H₂O = B"}, "cie-9701-2021-on-p12-q19": {"answer": "D", "solution": "Mg(OH)₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺ + 2OH⁻\nNH₄⁺ from NH₄Cl is a weak Brønsted–Lowry acid; it reacts with OH⁻ to give NH₃ + H₂O, removing OH⁻.\nRemoving OH⁻ shifts the dissolving equilibrium to the right, so Mg(OH)₂ dissolves.\nNaCl provides no acidic ion, so Mg(OH)₂ does not dissolve in NaCl solution.\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-2022-mj-p13-q13": {"answer": "B", "solution": "By definition, a Brønsted–Lowry acid is a proton (H⁺) donor and a Brønsted–Lowry base is a proton acceptor.\nThis is the statement that is ALWAYS correct; the other options add conditions that are not universal.\n→ B"}, "cie-9701-2021-on-p12-q32": {"answer": "C", "solution": "Four 0.1 mol dm⁻³ solutions: CH₃CO₂H (pH 4), HNO₃ (pH 1), CH₃NH₂ (pH 11), NaOH (pH 14).\n1. \"Acid 2 has a lower pH because it is more soluble\" ✗ — pH depends on dissociation, not solubility.\n2. NaOH (pH 14) has a higher OH⁻ concentration than CH₃NH₂ (pH 11) ✓.\n3. CH₃CO₂H (weak) dissociates less than HNO₃ (strong) ✓.\n→ 2 and 3 correct = C"}, "cie-9701-2022-on-p11-q23": {"answer": "C", "solution": "Ammonia is a Brønsted–Lowry base: it accepts a proton to form the ammonium ion.\nThe correct statement is that ammonia reacts with / accepts a proton from a strong acid (C).\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-2024-mj-p12-q22": {"answer": "D", "solution": "The ammonium ion NH₄⁺ can donate a proton (H⁺), so it acts as a Brønsted–Lowry acid (its conjugate base is NH₃).\nThe option describing this property is D.\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-2025-mj-p14-q25": {"answer": "D", "solution": "NH₄⁺ has three ordinary N–H covalent bonds plus one dative (coordinate) covalent bond, in which the nitrogen lone pair forms the bond to H⁺.\nThe option describing the dative bond in the ammonium ion is D.\n→ D"}, "cie-9701-2023-mj-p13-q16": {"answer": "A", "solution": "Titration curve: starts at high pH, ends at low pH, with a single steep drop at pH 7 (equivalence point).\nThis is the classic strong-base-titrated-with-strong-acid curve:\nX (flask) = NaOH(aq) (strong base), Y (burette) = HCl(aq) (strong acid).\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2023-on-p11-q18": {"answer": "A", "solution": "Bromocresol green: pH < 3.8 yellow, 3.8–5.4 green, > 5.4 blue.\nThe chloride of element T turns it yellow in water → acidic solution (pH < 3.8), so T forms an acidic (hydrolysing) chloride.\nAdding solid oxide of U turns the indicator yellow → green → blue → the acid is neutralised and pH rises, so U's oxide is a BASIC oxide.\nPick the pair with an acidic chloride and a basic oxide (A).\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2025-mj-p11-q16": {"answer": "C", "solution": "NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O\n1. \"The ammonium ions are reduced\" ✗ — no electrons are transferred (N's oxidation state is unchanged).\n2. In the reverse reaction NH₃ accepts H⁺ from H₂O, acting as a Brønsted–Lowry base ✓.\n3. NH₄⁺ and NH₃ have the same bond angle ✗ — NH₄⁺ is tetrahedral (109.5°), NH₃ is pyramidal (≈107°).\n4. This is NOT a redox reaction ✓.\n→ 2 and 4 correct = C"}, "cie-9701-2025-mj-p11-q9": {"answer": "B", "solution": "HQ(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + Q⁻(aq) (weak acid)\nAdding NaQ raises [Q⁻].\nBy Le Chatelier the equilibrium shifts left → [H⁺] falls → pH rises.\n→ [H⁺] decreases, pH increases = B"}, "cie-9701-2025-mj-p12-q21": {"answer": "A", "solution": "NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂ → 2NH₃ + CaCl₂ + 2H₂O (ionic: 2NH₄⁺ + 2OH⁻ → 2NH₃ + 2H₂O)\nThe ammonium ion donates a proton to OH⁻, so NH₄⁺ acts as a Brønsted–Lowry acid.\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-2021-mj-p12-q14": {"answer": "C", "solution": "To raise the pH of acidic soil (5.5 → above 6.0) you need a BASIC (alkaline) solid that neutralises H⁺ in the soil.\nSuitable basic compounds are metal oxides / hydroxides / carbonates (e.g. CaO, Ca(OH)₂, CaCO₃).\nThe option that is a basic solid is C.\n→ C"}, "cie-9701-2024-on-p12-q13": {"answer": "A", "solution": "Ca₅(PO₄)₃OH(s) ⇌ 5Ca²⁺ + 3PO₄³⁻ + OH⁻\nTo increase the amount of solid, shift the equilibrium LEFT by increasing [OH⁻].\nNH₃ is a base; adding it raises [OH⁻] → equilibrium shifts left → more solid forms.\nNH₄Cl and CH₃CO₂H are acidic and remove OH⁻ (dissolving the solid); NaCl has no effect.\n→ A (add NH₃)"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-11": {"answer": "D", "solution": "Comparing equal-concentration acids: sulfuric acid is a strong DIPROTIC acid.\n0.01 mol dm⁻³ H₂SO₄ releases about 0.02 mol dm⁻³ of H⁺ (two protons per molecule).\nThat is a higher [H⁺] than a monoprotic strong acid at the same concentration (only 0.01 mol dm⁻³ H⁺), so H₂SO₄ has the lowest pH.\n→ D (sulfuric acid)"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-12": {"answer": "C", "solution": "Ammonia + chlorine: 8NH₃ + 3Cl₂ → N₂ + 6NH₄Cl\n· Chlorine is reduced from 0 to −1 → chlorine is the oxidising agent (reduced).\n· Ammonia accepts H⁺ (acts as a base) and part of it is oxidised to N₂.\nOnly two of the eight N atoms are oxidised to N₂; the other six stay in NH₄⁺, so \"all oxidised\" is wrong.\n→ 2 and 3 correct = C"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-13": {"answer": "D", "solution": "Ammonia acts as a Brønsted–Lowry acid only when it DONATES a proton.\nReaction 1: one NH₃ donates a proton to another NH₃ (forming NH₂⁻ and NH₄⁺) → one ammonia acts as an acid ✓.\nReactions 2 and 3: ammonia accepts H⁺ or donates a lone pair, so it acts as a base, not an acid.\n→ only reaction 1 → D"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-14": {"answer": "B", "solution": "In the ammonia + chlorine reaction:\n1. Part of the ammonia is oxidised to N₂ → ammonia is a reducing agent ✓.\n2. Ammonia accepts H⁺ → ammonia acts as a base ✓.\n3. \"Hydrogen's oxidation state changes\" ✗ — hydrogen stays at +1 throughout.\n→ 1 and 2 correct = B"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-15": {"answer": "A", "solution": "Both reactions transfer only a proton (acid–base), with no electron transfer → neither is redox.\nSo SO₂ is not an oxidising agent, and SO₃²⁻ is not a reducing agent here.\nHSO₃⁻ is the conjugate base of sulfurous acid H₂SO₃: in the reverse of the first reaction it accepts H⁺, acting as a Brønsted–Lowry base.\n→ A"}, "cie-9701-qls-eq-mcq-16": {"answer": "D", "solution": "The reaction is a proton transfer: NH₄⁺ donates a proton to NH₂⁻, forming two NH₃.\nStatement 1 (\"NH₄⁺ is a proton donor and NH₂⁻ is a proton acceptor\") explains the acid–base classification.\nThe other statements, though true, do not explain WHY it is an acid–base reaction.\n→ D"}};
